# Thunder Den TODO

## Project rules

- Use simple English in code messages, comments, and documents.
- Keep the build easy to read and run.
- Install only files used by the final system.
- Keep support for legacy BIOS and UEFI systems.
- Keep GRUB because the hybrid image needs it for legacy BIOS support.
- Keep secrets in RAM and keep network and storage support disabled.

## 1. Add signing policy and user verification

- Use Bitcoin Core `decodepsbt` and `analyzepsbt` as the source of truth; do not implement another PSBT parser.
- Add `jq` only for safe extraction of Core's JSON. Keep shell and TUI code limited to policy, display, and confirmation.
- Keep `bitcoin-bash-tools` for BIP39 and key derivation, which Bitcoin Core does not provide.
- Before asking for the mnemonic, show Core's transaction outputs, amounts, fee, fee rate, locktime, and sighash rules.
- Require Core to report every input UTXO and a calculable fee, then require clear user confirmation.
- For the current single-signature BIP84 scope, accept only the default or `ALL` sighash and require `complete=true` after signing.
- Pass private descriptors through `bitcoin-cli -stdin`; pass mnemonics and passphrases through standard input, not arguments or exported variables.
- Keep testnet as the default while the project is experimental.

## 2. Update and pin the build

- Keep all main versions in one small, easy-to-read file.
- Update Buildroot to `2026.05.2`.
- Update Bitcoin Core to `31.1`.
- Update the build container to Debian `13`.
- Use the Linux kernel selected by Buildroot `2026.05.2` and review the final kernel configuration.
- Keep `bitcoin-bash-tools` at its current commit unless upstream changes.
- Store and check the SHA-256 hash of every downloaded source.
- Check the Buildroot signing-key fingerprint instead of trusting a key URL from the downloaded signature.
- Use a clean Buildroot output directory when a version or package setting changes.
- Keep download caches, but do not reuse target files for release builds.

## 3. Keep the image small

- Build an exact list of installed programs and libraries.
- Fail the build if an unexpected program or library is installed.
- Check again for unused Boost libraries after updating Bitcoin Core.
- Keep only QR formats that are needed. Prefer base64 plus one multipart format such as BBQR.
- Remove the other QR decoders after the supported multipart format is chosen.
- Review whether Bash, GNU coreutils, OpenSSL, and `dc` can be reduced without writing new untested cryptographic code.
- Review the final kernel configuration for unused drivers and features.
- Keep drivers needed for old BIOS-era computers, USB keyboards, and USB cameras.
- Keep Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, wired network, USB storage, disk storage, and kernel modules disabled.
- Document every direct and indirect runtime dependency in simple English.

## 4. Keep the build simple

- Keep `scripts/build/` separate from `scripts/runtime/`.
- Keep one documented Docker command as the normal build path.
- Put the builder definition in a small visible Dockerfile instead of hiding it inside a long shell script.
- Verify Buildroot before extracting it.
- Make the build create both boot images and their hashes without manual steps.

## 5. Test the real system

- Run the actual Bitcoin Core signing path against every PSBT test vector.
- Verify signatures, not only the presence of signature fields.
- Test wrong mnemonics, missing inputs, unsafe sighash types, and invalid PSBT data.
- Test malformed and incomplete QR input without unbounded memory use.
- Boot-test the hybrid image with both legacy BIOS and UEFI firmware.
- Boot-test the small image with x86_64 UEFI firmware.
- Test a real USB keyboard and several common USB cameras.
- Verify that root is read-only and RAM-backed, `/tmp` and `/run` are safe tmpfs mounts, and swap is off.
- Verify that no network, radio, host disk, or USB storage device is usable.
- Compare two clean builds and record any files that are not reproducible.

## 6. Finish release verification

- Include the repository commit, Buildroot configuration, kernel configuration, package list, and source hashes in the build manifest.
- Include final image hashes in one signed `SHA256SUMS` file.
- Build releases from a clean output directory.
- Inspect the final initramfs before each release.

## Future mnemonic generation

- Move `scripts/todo/thunderden_entropy_guard.sh` into `scripts/runtime/` before adding mnemonic generation.
- Run it before any mnemonic is generated.
- Test that it waits for the kernel random generator.
- Test that it fails when the required kernel settings are missing.

The entropy script is not installed yet because Thunder Den currently imports a mnemonic instead of generating one.
